"text": "The most honest first line in literature, I am a sick man, I am a spiteful man, I am an unattractive man, a man who spent forty years in his own head. Dostoyevsky's narrator has thought about everything and done almost nothing. He calls his mind a disease. Overthinking feels like depth. It's often just hiding. Every plan you analyze instead of starting is another night in the underground. Pick one thing you've thought about for weeks. Do the smallest version of it tomorrow before you can think again. What are you overthinking right now? Follow for sourced philosophy.",
